      "findings": [
        {
          "id": "F1-RA-001",
          "severity": "blocking",
          "blockingAuthority": "author",
          "title": "bounded legacy cutover anchor can be bypassed or moved forward",
          "problem": "The Apply implements the F1 legacy exemption by matching the E2 change identity instead of binding one stable original strict cutover boundary. readGitBoundarySummaries also special-cases an E2 subject-only candidate as a checkpoint fact. As a result, one consumer can activate post-E2 behavior from a non-strict checkpoint, while FormalFactReader can later select a newer duplicate E2 checkpoint and retroactively extend the legacy-readable window past the original strict anchor.",
          "contractRef": "openspec/changes/archive-and-checkpoint-boundary/design.md §3 (bounded legacy cutover) + specs/flowkit-archive-and-checkpoint-boundary/spec.md Historical checkpoint compatibility",
          "invariant": "Only checkpoints strictly before the original strict-admitted E2 cutover may use the bounded legacy read path; the anchor itself and every descendant/future checkpoint must remain strict, and no consumer may treat a subject-only E2 candidate as the activation checkpoint.",
          "requiredOutcome": "Bind the migration cutover to a stable strict E2 boundary and make all consumers respect that same strict activation invariant. Remove the subject-only E2 checkpoint exception from any current checkpoint fact/activation surface. Ensure later same-change commits cannot move the cutover forward (for example by deterministically selecting/pinning the original strict E2 anchor or failing closed on ambiguity). Preserve read-only compatibility only for true ancestors of that original anchor and keep the current base e182 strict transition valid.",
          "acceptance": [
            "A subject-only E2 checkpoint is not returned/consumed as a current formal activation checkpoint by readGitBoundarySummaries or isPostE2ThreeFileWriterActive.",
            "After an original strict E2 anchor exists, adding a later strict E2 checkpoint cannot make any descendant of the original anchor eligible for legacy checkpoint admission.",
            "The real e182 strict E2 checkpoint still activates the prospective three-file writer and true pre-E2 historical checkpoints remain readable without history rewrite.",
            "Fresh/downstream repositories remain strict by default and no new Delivery-local or subject-only fallback is introduced.",
            "Regression tests cover both counterexamples: subject-only E2 activation and later duplicate E2 with an intervening malformed post-anchor checkpoint."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
